ible Next Steps:\n1. **Transaction Magnitude Normalization**: Use z-score normalization for "credit_amount" to standardize magnitude values across diverse scales.\n2. **Employment Weighted Duration Impact**: Add a weighted feature that interacts "duration" with "employment_encoded" for modeling how stable employment affects longer credit durations.\n3. **Credit History Severity Index**: Create a numerical representation for "credit_history" based on the severity of the categories.\n\n---\n\n```python\n# 1. Transaction Magnitude Normalization\n# Feature description: Apply z-score normalization to "credit_amount" to standardize credit request amounts relative to the dataset\'s mean and standard deviation.\n# Usefulness: This feature addition helps mitigate scale-related biases that may interfere with model learning and ensures comparability of credit amounts across a wide range.\ndf[\'credit_amount_zscore\'] = (df[\'credit_amount\'] - df[\'credit_amount\'].mean()) / (df[\'credit_amount\'].std())\n```end\n\n```python\n# 2. Employment Weighted Duration Impact\n# Feature description: Create a feature that models the impact of employment stability on credit duration by multiplying "duration" with "employment_encoded".\n# Usefulness: This feature adds a layer of interaction between long-term employment and credit duration, which can reveal patterns in repayment probability.\ndf[\'employment_duration_impact\'] = df[\'duration\'] * df[\'employment_encoded\']\n```end\n\n```python\n# 3. Credit History Severity Index\n# Feature description: Map categorical "credit_history" values to numerical indices based on their severity (e.g., "critical/other existing credit" = 2, "delayed previously" = 3).\n# Usefulness: This transformation gives an ordinal ranking to "credit_history" that directly quantifies risk associated with each category.\ncredit_history_mapping = {\n    \'all paid\': 0,\n    \'no credits/all paid\': 0,\n    \'existing paid\': 1,\n    \'critical/other existing credit