es a log transformation to the credit_amount column to decrease the effect of extreme values and normalize the data distribution.\n# Usefulness: Credit amounts are highly skewed, with extreme outliers that could disproportionately influence predictions. A logarithmic transformation reduces this effect, thereby improving model robustness.\ndf[\'log_credit_amount\'] = df[\'credit_amount\'].apply(lambda x: np.log1p(x))  # log(1 + x) to avoid log(0) issues\n```end\n\n```python\n# Explanation why the column employment_duration_ratio is dropped\n# This column is being dropped because of extreme skewness (mean of 290,000 and std of 816,707), which could destabilize the model. The information in this column has already been incorporated via grouped aggregations like job_employment_mean.\ndf.drop(columns=[\'employment_duration_ratio\'], inplace=True)\n```end', "```python\n# Feature name: `interaction_checking_employment`\n# Feature description: This feature creates an interaction term between `checking_status` (label-encoded) and `employment` (label-encoded). Since checking_status represents the customer's financial liquidity and employment provides stability information, their interaction can give critical insight into the customer's creditworthiness (e.g., customers with low checking account balances but stable employment could be treated differently than those with unstable employment).\n# Usefulness: Combines liquidity and employment stability as a joint feature, which can help downstream models discern risk patterns more effectively. Interaction features often help capture non-linear relationships that individual features may miss.\n\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import LabelEncoder\nle = LabelEncoder()\n\ndf['checking_status_encoded'] = le.fit_transform(df['checking_status'])\ndf['employment_encoded'] = le.fit_transform(df['employment'])\ndf['interaction_checking_employment'] = df['checking_status_encoded'] * df['employment_encoded']\n```end\n\n```python\n# Feature name: